WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.820
C programs commonly receive command-line data through argc and argv.

2
00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:08.680
argc tells the program how many argument entries are available.

3
00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:11.500
argv provides pointers to argument strings.

4
00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:15.360
That model is simple, but it should not be confused with validation.

5
00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:20.260
Delivery by the operating environment does not prove that an argument has the expected meaning.

6
00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:24.700
Each argv entry is presented as a C string for the program to interpret.

7
00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:36.240
The program still needs to consider length, null termination expectations, encoding assumptions, and how the value will be copied, compared, parsed, or passed into internal logic.

8
00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:40.340
Treat the argument as input, not as a trusted internal object.

9
00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:46.540
The first argument is often treated as the program name, but assumptions can vary by environment and launcher.

10
00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:56.400
Code should avoid depending on the program-name value for security-sensitive decisions or precise behavior unless the surrounding platform contract is understood and reviewed.

11
00:00:56.680 --> 00:01:03.640
Platform and command processor behavior can affect how arguments are split, quoted, encoded, and delivered.

12
00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:12.940
A defensive parser should focus on the values it actually receives and should document its own accepted forms instead of assuming every launcher will represent input the same way.

13
00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:15.140
Pointer validity also has a scope.

14
00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:27.560
argv entries are valid during program execution under the normal startup contract, but copying those pointers into longer-lived or asynchronous contexts still needs lifetime review.

15
00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:34.700
A parser should produce a validated internal configuration rather than spreading raw argument pointers throughout the program.
